cartridge "MINES OF MERLIN/C.M.BLAKEMORE/1988"

' MINES OF MERLIN
' A ColecoVision game based on "Mines of Merlin" by Cleveland M.
' Blakemore, Ahoy! June 1988 (Commodore 64 BASIC; the listing's REM says
' "VAULT OF TERROR" V4.8, the title screen "MINES OF MERLIN", (C) 1988
' Nemesis Software). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: King Arthur's crown lies somewhere in the two levels of
' Merlin's mine. Walk the corridors in a first-person view, fight the
' creatures, take the relics they drop, open locked doors, go down the
' shafts on a rope and climb back up the ladders, and bring the crown
' back to the shaft you came down by (the start).
'
' Kept from the original (orig/merlin.txt): the 16 x 16 cell map, two
' levels of 16 x 8 (lines 4530-4680): corridors, coffins, shafts, ceiling
' exits, ladders, doors and their locks, each lock barring one direction;
' the crown at cell 204, the start at cell 17 facing south; the 16
' creatures of 8 kinds (1 1 2 2 3 3 4 5 5 on the upper level placed at
' random in cells 34-126, 6 6 6 7 7 7 8 on the lower in 142-237), each
' with X * 4 + 1 hit points; their move rule (towards the player by cell
' number: north when more than 6 below, south when more than 6 above,
' else east or west), one cell a tick, never onto another creature; the
' attack chance (5 + kind)% a turn, damage kind / (shield ? 5 : 1) +
' 1..3; the sword's miss when RND + LIFE / 100 < .5, its damage
' RND + 2 + LIFE / 5; a kill gives kind / 4 life and drops relic
' kind - 1; the wraith (kind 6) steals every relic but the torch, rope and
' crown and leaves them in its lair (cell 157, below the shaft at 29,
' locked on all four sides); the wand opens every lock of the cell ahead
' and strikes a wraith; the key opens a door's lock on your side; the
' rope goes down a shaft, up a ladder under a ceiling exit; life starts
' at 10, grows .4 a tick up to 60; the torch fades through five stages
' (6, 12, 18, 24, 30 ticks) and burns out; you cannot step back into a
' door. Win and loss screens with the original's words.
'
' Liberties: drawn in tiles with depth shading and stepped edges instead
' of PETSCII; creatures are smooth sprites in four sizes by distance;
' one tick is 8 seconds (the C64's 1000 jiffies were 16.7) and life
' grows every 2 seconds (the amulet doubles it) so that fights can be
' recovered from; life starts at 15, and a creature waits 3/4 of a
' second before its first attack (time to react); you start with the sword as well as the torch (in the
' original the only sword is a relic dropped by a creature that can only
' be killed with a sword); relics are picked up when you enter a cell
' (the C64 used a command bar); a burnt-out torch narrows the view to
' your own cell, and a spider's torch relights it; the ring makes you
' unseen for 10 seconds and then needs 40 to recharge (the C64's ring
' lasted for ever, which made the game safe); the win is bringing the
' crown to the start and using the rope there (the C64 asked for all
' nine relics at once, but its torches burn out); the wraith leaves you
' your sword (in the original a robbed player could be left with no way
' to fight or to reach the lair); creatures cry when
' they reach you; no save/load (no disk). Monster kinds are named
' spider, scorpion, rat, bat, goblin, wraith, serpent and naga (the
' listing names spiders, rats, goblins, serpents and the naga by their
' sounds).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): up walks forward, down steps back,
' left and right turn. Left button: use the selected relic (the sword
' attacks). Right button: select the next relic you carry.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers.

